  "summary": "This paper presents a design-based resampling benchmark evaluating whether revisiting reference locations (paired sampling) and incorporating model-assisted predictions from frozen classifiers trained on annual satellite embeddings (TESSERA and AlphaEarth Foundations) improve the estimation of year-to-year changes in the Shannon diversity of declared crop groups across 30 landscapes in Switzerland (SwissCrop25, 2021–2025). The study finds that: (1) direct paired sampling reduces mean squared error (MSE) by 72.5% overall at 128 labels/year compared to independent sampling (MSE ratio 0.275), driven strongly by persistent grassland-dominated landscapes (ratio 0.198) as opposed to arable-dominated landscapes (ratio 0.597) or pure rotating arable pixels where temporal influence covariance can even turn negative; (2) because true annual landscape-level diversity changes are very small (RMS change of 0.0260 entropy units), simply predicting zero change beats direct sampling at all budgets up to 512 labels/year and beats all estimators at 128 labels/year; (3) paired direct sampling suffers from severe 95% confidence interval undercoverage (83.4% overall, 78.9% in grassland landscapes), largely due to samples where zero sampled pixels change classes and plug-in standard errors collapse to zero; and (4) unweighted model-assisted difference estimators using frozen multinomial logistic classifiers fail to improve paired RMSE over direct sampling, require Euclidean projection onto the probability simplex in ~99% of draws, and only appear to have ~99% interval coverage when the simplex projection Jacobian is omitted.",
